<Doc
  title="CLI reference"
  description="Every command the exeora binary takes, and the flags worth knowing."
  canonicalPath="/docs/cli/"
>
  <p>
    Everything below <code>connect</code> is a piece you can run on its own.{" "}
    <code>connect</code> signs in and keeps the machine online; <code>project add</code> adds a
    directory or a repository as a project. Commands that act on a project take{" "}
    <code>--on</code> to say which <a href="/docs/projects/">location</a> they mean, so the same
    commands serve this machine, another machine of yours, and Exeora Cloud.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>connect</code></td>
        <td>Sign in, register this machine and keep it connected</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>login</code> / <code>logout</code></td>
        <td>Sign in, or forget the session on this machine. <code>login --code</code> is for machines without a browser.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>gateway</code> / <code>gateway use &lt;url&gt;</code> / <code>gateway reset</code></td>
        <td>Show or change which Exeora this machine talks to</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>device register</code> / <code>device list</code></td>
        <td>Register this machine by hand, or list your machines and the instances Exeora Cloud runs. <code>machine</code> is another name for <code>device</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project add [target]</code></td>
        <td>Add a directory or a repository as a project</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project list</code> / <code>project remove &lt;slug&gt;</code></td>
        <td>List your projects and where each one lives, or remove one everywhere it lives</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project locations &lt;slug&gt;</code> / <code>project locations add|remove</code></td>
        <td>List, add or remove the locations of a project</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project default &lt;slug&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Choose the location that calls naming no workspace land on</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project credential &lt;slug&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Replace or remove the repository token Exeora Cloud clones a project with</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>workspace create</code> / <code>workspace attach</code></td>
        <td>Create a workspace in any location, or connect a Git workspace that already exists on this machine</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>workspace list</code> / <code>workspace detach</code> / <code>workspace remove</code></td>
        <td>List workspaces and where each one lives, disconnect one, or remove one</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>config get|set|unset &lt;key&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Read or change <code>projects-root</code> and <code>workspace-root</code>, the two folders this machine keeps things in</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>init [path]</code></td>
        <td>Write an <code>exeora.toml</code> restricting what agents may do in a directory</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>prompt</code></td>
        <td>Print Exeora's coding-agent prompt, for a client that cannot fetch it itself</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>status</code></td>
        <td>Show registration, gateway and projects</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>logs</code></td>
        <td>Recent tool calls: what ran, which client asked, and how it ended</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sync</code></td>
        <td>Reconcile this machine's registration and projects with the dashboard</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>git-credential</code></td>
        <td>The credential helper git runs. Not a command for a person, and left out of <code>exeora --help</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>upgrade</code></td>
        <td>Update the installed binary to the latest release</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>connect</h2>

  <p>A few flags for the cases it cannot guess:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--name</code></td><td>Name the machine, when registering it</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--reset</code></td>
        <td>Register the machine again, after revoking it from the dashboard</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--gateway</code></td>
        <td>Serve to a different Exeora, and remember it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--code</code></td>
        <td>Sign in with a code shown in the terminal, for SSH and headless machines</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The first time <code>connect</code> runs at a terminal it asks where projects should be cloned
    on this machine, and saves the answer as <code>projects-root</code>. A machine that was told
    through <code>exeora config</code> or <code>EXEORA_PROJECTS_ROOT</code> is never asked, and
    neither is one running under <code>--json</code> or without a terminal.
  </p>

  <p>
    While <code>connect</code> is running, Exeora asks the operating system to keep both the machine
    and its display awake. The request covers relay reconnects and is released when the command
    exits. It does not override an explicit sleep request or closing a laptop lid. If the operating
    system cannot create the request, Exeora warns once and continues connecting.
  </p>

  <h2>login</h2>

  <p>
    <code>exeora login</code> opens a browser on this machine. On a server, a VM, or any SSH
    session where that cannot work, pass <code>--code</code>. The terminal prints a URL and a short
    code. Open the URL on a phone or laptop, type the code from the terminal, and approve Exeora.
    Do not paste a code you did not just see on a machine you control: that would sign their
    machine in as you. <code>exeora connect --code</code> does the same thing on the way to staying
    online.
  </p>

  <h2>gateway</h2>

  <p>
    Exeora is open source, so <code>https://exeora.dev</code> is a default rather than an address. <code>exeora gateway use https://your.example.com</code> points this machine at your own deployment and stores the choice, so every later command follows it with no flag and no variable. <code>exeora gateway</code> prints the active one and where it came from; <code>exeora gateway reset</code> goes back to the hosted one. <code>exeora login</code> and <code>exeora connect</code> take <code>--gateway</code> to do the same thing on the way past.
  </p>

  <p>
    One gateway is active at a time. Switching forgets the machine registration, the projects and the session belonging to the previous one, because a device id issued by one gateway's database means nothing to another. The CLI lists what it is about to forget and asks before it does. It also checks that a gateway actually answers at the address before touching anything, so a typo leaves a working setup exactly as it was.
  </p>

  <h2 id="project">project</h2>

  <pre>exeora project add
exeora project add ~/code/api
exeora project add owner/repo
exeora project add owner/repo --on cloud
exeora project add https://git.example.com/you/repo.git</pre>

  <p>
    <code>project add</code> takes a directory on this machine, the address of a repository, or{" "}
    <code>owner/repo</code> on GitHub. A directory is registered where it is; one that exists is
    read as a directory whatever else its name could mean. A repository is cloned into this
    machine's projects folder, or put on Exeora Cloud with <code>--on cloud</code>. With no
    target, the current directory is added. When it is not inside a checkout, the command runs at
    a terminal and <a href="/docs/github/">GitHub is connected</a>, a repository is picked from a
    list instead, and the command asks where it should live.
  </p>

  <p>
    What makes a checkout the same project on another machine is its remote. Adding a checkout
    whose remote is already a project of your account joins that project as one more location,
    and the command says so. A name that belongs to a different repository on another machine is
    refused with <code>slug_taken</code> rather than moved. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/projects/#second-machine">how a second machine joins a project</a>.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--name</code>, <code>--slug</code></td><td>Override what the directory or the repository implies</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--on &lt;machine|cloud&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Where a repository goes: this machine by default, or <code>cloud</code>. Naming another machine of yours adds it as a location of a project that already exists. A directory is always added from the machine it is on.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><code>--branch</code></td><td>The project's default branch; the repository's own by default</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--token-stdin</code>, <code>--username</code></td>
        <td>A repository token read from standard input, for a private repository on Exeora Cloud. Refused for a machine of yours, which clones with the GitHub connection of your account or its own git credentials.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><code>-y</code></td><td>Do not ask before cloning or before starting an instance</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <pre>exeora project list
exeora project locations api
exeora project locations add api --on desktop
exeora project locations remove api --on desktop
exeora project default api --on desktop
exeora project credential api --token-stdin &lt; token.txt
exeora project credential api --remove
exeora project remove api</pre>

  <p>
    <code>--on</code> takes a machine's name, that name as a slug, <code>here</code> for the
    machine the command runs on, or <code>cloud</code>. <code>project list</code> prints every
    project of the account with its repository, and under it each location with its state and its
    path, the default marked with <code>*</code>. <code>locations add</code> clones nothing: the
    first workspace made in that location brings the copy. <code>locations remove</code> and{" "}
    <code>project remove</code> ask first unless <code>-y</code> is given, and never delete files
    on your machines. Removing a machine of yours as a location forgets that copy and leaves every
    other location alone. Removing Exeora Cloud as a location, or removing the project, destroys
    its instances there.
  </p>

  <p>
    Only <code>project remove</code> removes a project. <code>locations remove</code> on the only location of a project that has a repository leaves the project, which <a href="/docs/projects/#nowhere">lives nowhere</a> until it is given a place again, and the command says how. <code>project list</code> prints such a project with <code>nowhere</code> in place of its locations, and Exeora Cloud that holds no instance for the project root with the state <code>no instance</code>. A directory with no remote cannot lose its only location: the command is refused and names <code>project remove</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>project credential</code> is for a repository that is not connected to GitHub. The token
    arrives on standard input, never as an argument, so it stays out of shell history and process
    lists. Instances created or retried from then on use it; one already running keeps the token
    it was set up with.
  </p>

  <h2 id="workspace">workspace</h2>

  <pre>exeora workspace create feature/name
exeora workspace create feature/name --from main
exeora workspace create feature/name --project api --on desktop
exeora workspace create feature/name --project api --on cloud</pre>

  <p>
    Without <code>--on</code>, or with <code>--on</code> naming this machine,{" "}
    <code>workspace create</code> makes a native Git worktree here, from the current workspace's{" "}
    <code>HEAD</code>, registers it under the project and synchronizes its stable id with the
    gateway. A project this machine is a location of and holds no copy of yet is cloned into the
    projects folder first. With <code>--on</code> naming another location the workspace is made
    there: a worktree on that machine, which has to be connected, or an instance on Exeora Cloud,
    which the command waits for.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use <code>--from &lt;ref&gt;</code> to choose another base, or{" "}
    <code>--reuse-existing-branch</code> when the branch already exists. <code>--project</code>{" "}
    selects a project when the command is not run inside one. <code>--path</code> places a
    worktree on this machine somewhere other than the default root, and cannot be combined with{" "}
    <code>--on</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>workspace list</code> shows the workspaces of the project the current directory is in,
    with the machine that holds each one, or of every project when the directory is in none.{" "}
    <code>--project</code> names another project and <code>--all</code> lists every project.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>attach</code> connects an existing workspace on this machine without taking ownership of
    its creation. <code>detach</code> only disconnects it from Exeora. <code>remove</code> runs
    Git's worktree removal and refuses uncommitted changes unless <code>--force</code> is present;
    branches are kept unless <code>--delete-branch</code> is requested, and then Git's safe{" "}
    <code>branch -d</code> is used. A workspace on Exeora Cloud is removed by destroying its
    instance, after asking unless <code>-y</code> is given. A workspace on another machine of
    yours is removed there, where its files are, and the command says which machine that is.
  </p>

  <p>
    A gateway outage leaves a safe pending record and <code>exeora sync</code> retries it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="config">config</h2>

  <pre>exeora config get projects-root
exeora config set projects-root ~/code/exeora
exeora config unset workspace-root</pre>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Key</th><th>What it is</th><th>Default</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>projects-root</code></td>
        <td>Where a repository is cloned when this machine has no copy of it</td>
        <td><code>~/exeora</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>workspace-root</code></td>
        <td>Where Exeora creates the worktrees of managed workspaces</td>
        <td>The platform's data directory</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>EXEORA_PROJECTS_ROOT</code> and <code>EXEORA_WORKSPACE_ROOT</code> override them for one
    environment. A relative path given to <code>set</code> is resolved against the current
    directory before it is stored.
  </p>

  <h2 id="device">device</h2>

  <p>
    <code>exeora device list</code>, or <code>exeora machine list</code>, prints every machine of
    the account: the ones you connected, with the projects each holds, and the instances Exeora
    Cloud runs, with the project and workspace each was made for. The machine the command runs on
    is marked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sync">sync</h2>

  <p>
    <code>exeora sync</code> brings this machine's configuration in line with what the gateway
    holds. It forgets a machine that was deleted from the dashboard, mirrors the projects this
    machine is a location of, and retries the workspace registrations and removals that a gateway
    outage left pending. It also tells Exeora the repository of projects the gateway knows only as
    a directory, which is every project added by a CLI older than 0.18.0. Run it once on such a
    machine: until the gateway knows the repository, the project cannot live anywhere else.
  </p>

  <p>
    What a deleted machine forgets is its own configuration. A project with a repository stays in the account, in its other locations or <a href="/docs/projects/#nowhere">nowhere</a>. Run <code>exeora connect</code> to register the machine again, then <code>exeora project add .</code> in a checkout to make it a location of its project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="git-credential">git-credential</h2>

  <pre>exeora git-credential [--project &lt;id&gt;] get|store|erase</pre>

  <p>
    A <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/gitcredentials" target="_blank" rel="noopener">git
    credential helper</a>, run by git rather than by you. For <code>get</code> it reads git's
    request from standard input and answers with a short-lived token for the repository of the
    project, made by the gateway for a project <a href="/docs/github/">connected to GitHub</a>.
    Without <code>--project</code> the project is found from the host and path git names, which
    needs <code>credential.useHttpPath</code> to be <code>true</code>. <code>store</code> and{" "}
    <code>erase</code> do nothing: the token is made on request and kept nowhere.
  </p>

  <p>
    A helper that fails must fail quietly. On any failure it prints nothing to standard output,
    exits with success and says why on standard error, so git moves on to the next helper. It
    answers only over https and only for the host git asked about.
  </p>

  <p>
    You do not configure it. When the CLI clones with this helper and the machine's own git could
    not have read the repository, it writes the helper into that repository's{" "}
    <code>.git/config</code>, for that host only, so later fetches and pushes work. Your global git
    configuration is never changed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cloud">cloud</h2>

  <p>
    Exeora Cloud has no commands of its own: it is a location, reached with{" "}
    <code>--on cloud</code> on the commands above. The older <code>exeora cloud</code> commands
    still work as aliases, so scripts written for them keep running. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cloud/">Exeora Cloud</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>exeora connect --cloud</code> is the other side: what an instance itself runs as a service. It reads a machine token from disk instead of signing in, holds the instance awake while it works, and answers the gateway's wake requests. Since 0.19.0 it also runs the project's <a href="/docs/cloud/#scripts">scripts</a> there, and <code>gh</code> on the instance is <a href="/docs/cloud/#gh">signed in as you</a>. There is no reason to run it on a laptop.
  </p>

  <h2>init</h2>

  <p>
    Interactive by default. Pass <code>--yes</code> to take the flags as given and ask nothing, and{" "}
    <code>--force</code> to replace a file that already exists.
  </p>

  <pre>exeora init --yes --mode allow_list --allow "npm,git *" --deny "sudo,rm *"</pre>

  <p>
    <code>--tools</code> takes the same comma-separated form. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/policy/">what a project allows</a> for what the values mean.
  </p>

  <h2>prompt</h2>

  <p>
    Writes the prompt to stdout and nothing else, so it pipes. Every MCP client already receives this
    over the wire; the command is for the ones that never ask. Pass <code>--account</code> for the
    variant that explains choosing between projects.
  </p>

  <pre>exeora prompt > AGENTS.md</pre>

  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/agent-prompt/">the agent prompt</a> for what it says and the other three ways
    to get it.
  </p>

  <h2>upgrade</h2>

  <p>
    <code>exeora upgrade</code> downloads the newest stable GitHub release for this operating
    system and CPU, verifies its published SHA-256 checksum, and safely replaces the running
    executable with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The upgrade needs write access to the executable. Installations made with the standard scripts
    live in a user-owned directory, so they do not normally need elevated privileges.
  </p>

  <h2>Machine-readable output</h2>

  <p>
    <code>--json</code> makes <code>status</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>device list</code>,{" "}
    <code>project list</code>, <code>project locations</code>, <code>workspace list</code> and <code>config get</code> print one JSON document instead of drawing on the terminal. Errors
    become JSON on stderr with a non-zero exit, so a caller piping stdout into a parser gets either
    valid JSON or nothing. Under <code>--json</code> nothing is asked, so a command that removes
    something or destroys an instance needs <code>-y</code>.
  </p>

  <pre>exeora logs --json | jq '.[] | select(.status == "error")'</pre>

  <p>
    Three of those documents changed shape when projects gained locations, because they stopped
    describing one machine and started describing the account:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>project list</code> prints the account's projects as the gateway lists them, not only
      the ones on this machine. Each carries <code>repoUrl</code>, <code>defaultBranch</code>,{" "}
      <code>mcpUrl</code> and <code>locations</code>, and each location its <code>slug</code>,{" "}
      <code>name</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>localPath</code> and whether
      it is the <code>default</code>. Since 0.18.1 each project also carries{" "}
      <code>nowhere</code>, true for one that has no default machine: its{" "}
      <code>locations</code> are then empty, or hold Exeora Cloud in state{" "}
      <code>no instance</code>. <code>project locations</code> carries the same key, and{" "}
      <code>project locations remove</code> answers with it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>device list</code> prints every machine with its <code>kind</code>,{" "}
      <code>local</code> or <code>cloud</code>, its <code>state</code> and{" "}
      <code>thisMachine</code>. A machine of yours lists the <code>projects</code> it holds; an
      instance names its <code>project</code> and <code>workspace</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>workspace list</code> prints every workspace of the listed projects wherever it is,
      with <code>machine</code>, <code>deviceId</code>, <code>cloud</code>,{" "}
      <code>projectSlug</code>, <code>thisMachine</code> and <code>syncState</code>. The project
      roots, which the table always shows, are added with <code>--roots</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Every key that 0.17.0 printed is still printed, with the value it had, beside the fields above.
    A path on another machine is <code>null</code> in those keys, so a script does not open a
    directory of the same name on this one.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>project add</code> answers with <code>project</code> and <code>location</code>, which is{" "}
    <code>created</code>, <code>joined</code> or <code>updated</code>, and with{" "}
    <code>adopted</code> when a repository was placed on this machine: true for a checkout that was
    already in the projects folder, false for a fresh clone.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>status</code> carries <code>accountMcpUrl</code> alongside each project's own{" "}
    <code>mcpUrl</code>, on both the signed-in and signed-out shapes, so a script can find the URL
    that covers every project without having to build it.
  </p>

  <p>
    On <code>connect</code>, which never finishes, it prints one JSON object per line as things
    happen, which is what a supervisor or a log collector can read as it arrives. In that mode there
    is nobody at the terminal, so confirmations go to the dashboard instead. The{" "}
    <code>awake</code> event reports whether system and display sleep prevention is active, with a{" "}
    <code>reason</code> when the operating system rejected the request.
  </p>

  <h2>Where things are stored</h2>

  <p>
    The refresh token goes to the OS keychain. Machines with no secret service, which is most Linux
    servers and CI containers, get a <code>0600</code> file under{" "}
    <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/exeora/</code> instead, and the CLI says so when it happens. Everything
    else is plain JSON; <code>exeora status</code> prints the path.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>EXEORA_GATEWAY_URL</code> points the CLI at a different gateway for one shell, without overwriting the stored value it outranks. For anything longer lived, including a self-hosted deployment you use every day, <code>exeora gateway use</code> is the one that sticks.
  </p>

  <h2>The one thing it asks for</h2>

  <p>
    On the third interactive run, if the GitHub CLI is installed and signed in, Exeora asks once whether to star the repository, and does it with <code>gh</code> if you say yes. It checks first whether you already starred it, in which case it never asks. Say no and it goes quiet for a long while, asks a second and last time, then never again.
  </p>

  <p>
    The question needs a real terminal, so it never appears under <code>--json</code>, in CI, in a script, or on a machine without <code>gh</code>. <code>EXEORA_NO_STAR_PROMPT=1</code> turns it off outright. Left unanswered for thirty seconds it takes the default, which is yes, and prints the one command that undoes it.
  </p>
</Doc>
